Q: Dependency list generated from import in TypeScript A: <p>This works:</p>
<pre><code>// Adopted from - https://stackoverflow.com/a/72128970
// Posted by denalisk

import * as fs from &quot;fs&quot;;
import * as ts from &quot;typescript&quot;;
import path from &quot;path&quot;;

function resolvePathsToModuleImports(tsConfigFile: string, tsFile: string) {
  // To use the typescript module resolution, you need to get the tsconfig
  const tsConfig = fs.readFileSync(tsConfigFile, &quot;utf8&quot;);
  const json = JSON.parse(tsConfig);
  const tsConfigObject = ts.parseJsonConfigFileContent(json, ts.sys, &quot;.&quot;);

  const tsFileText = fs.readFileSync(tsFile).toString();
  const fileInfo = ts.preProcessFile(tsFileText);

  const importFiles = [];
  const indexFiles = [];
  fileInfo.importedFiles
    .map((importedModule) =&gt; importedModule.fileName)
    .forEach((rawImport) =&gt; {
      const resolvedImport = ts.resolveModuleName(
        rawImport,
        tsFile,
        tsConfigObject.options,
        ts.sys
      );
      const importLoc = resolvedImport.resolvedModule.resolvedFileName;
      if (
        path.basename(importLoc) === &quot;index.ts&quot; &amp;&amp;
        !indexFiles.includes(importLoc)
      ) {
        indexFiles.push(importLoc);
        const recursedFiles = resolvePathsToModuleImports(tsConfigFile, importLoc);
        importFiles.push(...recursedFiles.filter((f) =&gt; !importFiles.includes(f)));
      } else if (!importFiles.includes(importLoc)) {
        importFiles.push(importLoc);
      }
    });
  return importFiles;
}

const tsConfigFile = process.argv[2];
const tsFile = process.argv[3];
console.log(resolvePathsToModuleImports(tsConfigFile, tsFile).join(&quot; &quot;));
</code></pre>

Q: How to access the values from two arrays related by associative keys with a single loop A: <pre><code>$nationality = array ('Green' =&gt; 'England', 'Dekker' =&gt; 'Holland','Bunting'=&gt; 'England', 'Montgomery'=&gt;'Scotland');

$ranking = array ('Green' =&gt; 4, 'Dekker' =&gt; 6,'Bunting'=&gt; 1, 'Montgomery'=&gt; 57);

foreach($nationality as $player =&gt; $country){
    echo "{$player} is from {$country} and is ranked {$ranking[$player]}. ";
}
</code></pre>

<p>This will output:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Green is from England and is ranked 4. Dekker is from Holland and is ranked 6. Bunting is from England and is ranked 1. Montgomery is from Scotland and is ranked 57.</p>
</blockquote>

Q: What is the most violent way that an application can terminate itself (Linux) A: <p>As pointed out, try to consume as many resources as possible until the kernel kills you:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>while(1)
{
    malloc(1);
    fork();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Another way is trying to write to a read-only page. Just keep writing memory until you get a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bus_error" rel="nofollow noreferrer">bus error</a>.</p>
<p>If you can get to the kernel, a great way to kill it is simply writing over a data structure the kernel uses. There are bonus points if you find a page as only readable and marked as writable and then overwrite it. BTW, most Linux kernels allow writing to the syscall_table or interrupt table. If you write there your system will crash for sure.</p>

Q: PHP Foreach with two or more arrays A: <p>This should work for you:</p>

<p>(Here I just go through all 3 arrays with <a href="http://php.net/manual/en/function.array-map.php" rel="nofollow"><code>array_map()</code></a> an print them in the structure you want)</p>

<pre><code>&lt;?php

    array_map(function($v1, $v2, $v3){
        echo "&lt;div&gt;";
            echo "&lt;p&gt;$v1&lt;/p&gt;";
            echo "&lt;p&gt;$v2&lt;/p&gt;";
            echo "&lt;p&gt;$v3&lt;/p&gt;";
        echo "&lt;/div&gt;";
    }, $boardType, $hotelPrice, $hotelName);

?&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Example input/output:</p>

<pre><code>$boardType = [1,2,3];
$hotelPrice = [4,5,6];
$hotelName = [7,8,9];
</code></pre>

